You wake on a straw-stuffed mattress, throat dry and head light. A single beam of morning slips through the cracked shutter and lands across your boots, stirring dust motes that drift like tired flies.

A clay cup waits on the bedside plank. You reach for it; the cup skitters an inch, then stops. You stare, blink, decide the motion was your own half-awake tug. The room smells of last night’s hearth smoke and damp straw, nothing more.

Outside, iron cart wheels clatter over cobbles, a hawker hollers prices, and somewhere a dog barks at a toppled crate. Laughter follows the crash, then the low clang of the market bell calling the day to order.

Your pack leans by the door, cloak draped over it. The narrow landing creaks under passing feet, and the innkeeper’s voice drifts up, greeting early guests. What do you do?

